{
  "image_name": "0ab4ee09cd62c55488fd23bda67cd68b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how lysine acetylation is generated, removed, and linked to cellular acetyl‑CoA metabolism. At the top, a lysine side chain is shown being acetylated by transfer of an acetyl group from acetyl‑CoA, either enzymatically by lysine acetyltransferases or, in some cases, non‑enzymatically, producing acetyl‑lysine. The reverse reaction is catalyzed by two deacetylase systems: HDACs, which use water and release acetate, and sirtuins, which consume NAD+ and generate deacetylated lysine plus an acetylated ADP‑ribose product.\n\nThe center connects this chemistry to metabolism in mitochondria, cytoplasm, and nucleus. Glucose enters glycolysis to form pyruvate, which is converted by PDC to acetyl‑CoA in mitochondria. Fatty acid β‑oxidation also feeds mitochondrial acetyl‑CoA, while citrate exported from the TCA cycle is converted back to acetyl‑CoA by ACLY in the cytoplasm and nucleus. ACSS2 appears to regenerate acetyl‑CoA from acetate, and ACC1/ACC2 channel acetyl‑CoA into malonyl‑CoA for fatty acid synthesis.\n\nThe bottom panels organize the main enzyme families involved: several acetyltransferase groups and deacetylase classes, including HDACs and NAD‑dependent sirtuins, with colored dots indicating whether these proteins are found in the nucleus, cytoplasm, nucleolus, or mitochondria.",
